course: dictionary
lesson: 1342
title: Campaigners: people who fight for change
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A campaigner is a person who works hard to change something in society or politics. They might want cleaner rivers, safer roads or a new law. Campaigners often gather people together, speak out and ask those in charge to listen.
explain: Read the clues around the word. «The rules have changed before: your great-great-gran may not have been allowed to vote just because she was a woman, until campaigners called suffragettes and suffragists won that right.» The rules changed because people campaigned, so a campaigner must be someone who works to make things change.
explain: The word also has older meanings. An «old campaigner» can be a soldier who has fought in many campaigns, or someone with lots of experience. Use the context to choose the right meaning: is the text about rules and society, or about soldiers and long experience?
fact: A campaigner is a person who works hard to change something in society or politics.
fact: Campaigners may protect the environment, change a law or make children safer.
fact: Greta Thunberg and Malala Yousafzai are famous young campaigners.
fact: Suffragettes and suffragists were campaigners who won women the right to vote.
fact: «An old campaigner» can mean a soldier who has fought in many campaigns, or someone with lots of experience.
exercise: «Campaigners» called for a new law to keep children safe near busy roads. What is a campaigner? | *A person who works hard to change something in society or politics | A person who drives a camper van on holiday | A soldier who has never fought | A person who reads the news on television
exercise: Sofia says, «An old campaigner knows the rules well.» What does old campaigner mean here? | A child who is sleepy | *Someone with lots of experience | A very tall tree | A brand-new student
exercise: Which words in «until campaigners called suffragettes and suffragists won that right» show that campaigners changed the rules? | great-great-gran | your | *won that right | may not
test: Which person is a campaigner? | A boy who wins a race | *A man who gathers people to protest against litter in the park | A dog who guards the house | A woman who collects stamps
test: Malala Yousafzai is a famous young campaigner. What does this tell you about her? | She is a famous runner | *She works hard to change things in society | She is a famous chef | She is a famous farmer
exam: Which sentence uses campaigner correctly, to mean someone who fights for change? | The campaigner baked a cake for her friend's birthday. | The campaigner was sleeping under a tree in the garden. | The campaigner fixed my bike chain. | *The campaigners handed out leaflets to ask the council for a safer crossing.
